---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import GradeCalculator from '@components/islands/GradeCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'grade-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate my weighted grade?', answer: 'Multiply each assessment\'s score by its weight, add those up, and divide by the total weight. The "Weighted average" mode does this — add a row for each assignment, test or project with its score and weight.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate a weighted average?', answer: 'Weight each score by its share of the grade. A quiz at 80% (worth 20%), a midterm at 75% (30%) and a final at 90% (50%) give 0.20×80 + 0.30×75 + 0.50×90 = 83.5%. The heavier the weight, the more that score moves the result.' },
  { question: 'What grade do I need on the final?', answer: 'Switch to "Final grade needed" mode. Enter your current grade, how much the final is worth, and your target grade, and the calculator tells you the score you need on the final.' },
  { question: 'What if the needed score is over 100%?', answer: 'It means your target is not reachable with the final alone. The calculator flags this so you can set a realistic goal.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between a course grade and a GPA?', answer: 'A course grade is your percentage in one class, weighted by assessments. A GPA converts final letter grades across several courses to points (on a 4.0 scale) and averages them by credit hours. Use the GPA calculator for the second.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  rail
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-04' }}
  intro="Weight-average your coursework, or find what you need on the final."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <GradeCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Two grade tools in one</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>Grade Calculator</strong> weight-averages the work you have done. Grades can be percentages (88) or letters (B+), mixed freely, and the optional planning block says what the work still to come has to average.</li>
      <li>The <strong>Final Grade Calculator</strong> answers the other question: given where you stand and what the final is worth, what do you need on it?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the average is worked out</h2>
    <p>
      Every grade is read as a letter first. A percentage lands in the band it falls in — 90 is an
      A−, 88 is a B+ — and that letter becomes grade points. The <strong>grade points</strong> are
      then weighted by each item's share of the course, which is why a percentage and a letter can
      sit in the same column and still be averaged together.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example you can check</h2>
    <p>
      Homework 1 scored 90 and is worth 5%; a project scored B and is worth 20%; a midterm scored 88
      and is worth 20%. Those become 3.7, 3.0 and 3.3 grade points, and weighting them over the 45%
      entered gives (3.7×5 + 3.0×20 + 3.3×20) ÷ 45 = <strong>3.21</strong>, which is nearest to a
      <strong>B+</strong>. The calculator reports it as <strong>B+ (3.21)</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note that the total is 45%, not 100%. The average is over the work entered, so it tells you
      where you stand now rather than what the course will finish at — which is what the planning
      block and the final grade calculator are for.
    </p>

    <h2>What you need on the final</h2>
    <p>
      If your current grade is 88, you want to finish on 85, and the final is worth 40% of the
      course, then the other 60% has already banked 0.6 × 88 = 52.8 points of the 85 you need. The
      final has to supply the remaining 32.2 across its 40%, so you need 32.2 ÷ 0.4 =
      <strong>80.5</strong> or higher.
    </p>
    <p>
      Enter both grades as percentages and the answer is a percentage; enter both as letters and the
      answer comes back in grade points with its letter. Keep the two on the same scale — a
      percentage against a letter would be comparing different things.
    </p>

    <h2>When the target is not reachable</h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes the number that comes back is above 100, or above the 4.3 an A+ is worth. That is a
      real answer, not an error: it means the target cannot be reached from where you are, and the
      calculator says so rather than quietly capping the figure. The same goes the other way — if
      even a zero on the final leaves you above your target, it tells you the grade is already
      secured.
    </p>

    <h2>Letter grades and their numerical equivalents</h2>
    <p>These are the grade points and percentage ranges both calculators use.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Letter Grade</th><th>GPA</th><th>Percentage</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A+</td><td>4.3</td><td>97–100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A</td><td>4</td><td>93–96%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A−</td><td>3.7</td><td>90–92%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B+</td><td>3.3</td><td>87–89%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B</td><td>3</td><td>83–86%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B−</td><td>2.7</td><td>80–82%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C+</td><td>2.3</td><td>77–79%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C</td><td>2</td><td>73–76%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C−</td><td>1.7</td><td>70–72%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D+</td><td>1.3</td><td>67–69%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D</td><td>1</td><td>63–66%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D−</td><td>0.7</td><td>60–62%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F</td><td>0</td><td>0–59%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Grading systems differ between countries, schools and even departments — some do not award an
      A+ or a B−, and the percentage boundaries move. Check your own syllabus if these ranges do not
      match it. For a whole transcript rather than one course, use the
      <a href="/everyday/gpa-calculator">GPA calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Understanding weights</h2>
    <p>
      A weight is the share of the final grade an item carries, so a 20% midterm moves your average
      four times as much as a 5% homework. Weights do not have to add to 100 while a course is in
      progress: the calculator averages over whatever you have entered and shows that total, so you
      can see how much of the course the figure actually covers.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
